WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:05.468
Every program you've written so far runs straight down the page.

00:00:05.468 --> 00:00:08.860
Line 1, line 2, line 3, each exactly once.

00:00:09.210 --> 00:00:11.778
But think about the apps you use every day.

00:00:11.778 --> 00:00:14.167
If the password is wrong, show an error.

00:00:14.167 --> 00:00:17.810
For every student in the class, add their score to the total.

00:00:18.160 --> 00:00:20.380
Real programs need two more things.

00:00:20.380 --> 00:00:25.960
They need to choose which lines to run, and they need to run some lines again and again.

00:00:26.310 --> 00:00:29.474
Here's the smallest example of each.

00:00:29.474 --> 00:00:30.089
x is 5.

00:00:30.089 --> 00:00:33.253
If x is less than 10, print smaller.

00:00:33.253 --> 00:00:36.592
If x is greater than 20, print bigger.

00:00:36.592 --> 00:00:38.350
Then print finished.

00:00:38.700 --> 00:00:42.419
5 less than 10 is True, so smaller is printed.

00:00:42.419 --> 00:00:46.705
5 greater than 20 is False, so that print is skipped.

00:00:46.705 --> 00:00:50.020
The output is smaller, and then finished.

00:00:50.370 --> 00:00:52.056
And here's a loop.

00:00:52.056 --> 00:00:56.929
While n is greater than 0, print n, and take 1 away.

00:00:56.929 --> 00:01:00.770
It prints 5, 4, 3, 2, 1, and then Finish.

00:01:01.120 --> 00:01:06.400
The rules that decide which line runs next are called flow control.

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:12.280
Welcome to Lecture 3 of Introduction to AI Programming: Flow Control.

00:01:12.630 --> 00:01:14.527
This lecture has 4 parts.

00:01:14.527 --> 00:01:19.840
In this first part, we'll see how a program asks a yes or no question,

00:01:19.840 --> 00:01:24.470
and how it uses the answer to choose, with if, else and elif.

00:01:25.267 --> 00:01:29.774
Every decision starts with a question whose answer is yes or no.

00:01:29.774 --> 00:01:35.267
In Python, that's a Boolean expression, and its value is either True or False.

00:01:35.617 --> 00:01:40.550
The simplest Boolean expressions use the 6 comparison operators:

00:01:40.550 --> 00:01:43.864
less than, less than or equal to, equal to,

00:01:43.864 --> 00:01:48.257
greater than or equal to, greater than, and not equal to.

00:01:48.607 --> 00:01:52.447
Notice that equal to is written with two equals signs.

00:01:52.797 --> 00:01:55.681
One equals sign is assignment.

00:01:55.681 --> 00:02:00.488
x equals 5 makes the name x refer to the object 5.

00:02:00.488 --> 00:02:03.565
Two equals signs ask a question.

00:02:03.565 --> 00:02:09.237
x double equals 5 is True or False, and it changes nothing.

00:02:09.837 --> 00:02:12.450
Python keeps the two apart for you.

00:02:12.450 --> 00:02:16.408
If you write if x equals 5 with a single equals sign,

00:02:16.408 --> 00:02:21.037
Python stops with a syntax error before the program even runs.

00:02:21.834 --> 00:02:26.898
Here's a program that asks 6 questions about the same x, which is 5.

00:02:26.898 --> 00:02:33.154
Each if is tested on its own, so every message whose condition is True gets printed.

00:02:33.504 --> 00:02:35.834
5 equals 5: True.

00:02:35.834 --> 00:02:38.987
5 greater than 4: True.

00:02:38.987 --> 00:02:43.784
5 greater than or equal to 5: True.

00:02:44.134 --> 00:02:46.396
5 less than 5? That's False.

00:02:46.396 --> 00:02:48.496
5 is not less than itself.

00:02:48.496 --> 00:02:52.454
So Less than 5 is the one message that's missing.

00:02:52.804 --> 00:02:55.812
5 less than or equal to 5: True.

00:02:55.812 --> 00:02:58.632
And 5 is not equal to 6: True.

00:02:58.632 --> 00:03:00.324
5 lines of output.

00:03:00.674 --> 00:03:03.203
Comparisons work on strings too.

00:03:03.203 --> 00:03:07.707
Hello is greater than Bye, and AB is not greater than AC.

00:03:07.707 --> 00:03:13.634
Strings are compared letter by letter, and we'll see exactly how in part 4.

00:03:14.434 --> 00:03:19.448
True and False are the 2 values of Python's Boolean type, called bool.

00:03:19.448 --> 00:03:25.394
It's named after George Boole, the mathematician who invented the algebra of logic.

00:03:25.744 --> 00:03:28.506
They're values like any other.

00:03:28.506 --> 00:03:35.504
Passed equals 75 greater than or equal to 60 stores True in the name passed.

00:03:35.854 --> 00:03:41.569
In fact, Python lets you use any value where it needs a yes or no answer.

00:03:41.569 --> 00:03:45.014
The function bool shows how a value is read.

00:03:45.364 --> 00:03:51.628
0, of every number type, and every empty collection, count as False.

00:03:51.628 --> 00:03:54.484
Everything else counts as True.

00:03:55.084 --> 00:04:00.088
So bool of 0 is False, and bool of an empty list is False.

00:04:00.088 --> 00:04:06.042
But bool of a list holding a 0 is True, because the list isn't empty.

00:04:06.042 --> 00:04:09.752
And the string '0', in quotes, is True too.

00:04:09.752 --> 00:04:11.564
It holds 1 character.

00:04:11.914 --> 00:04:15.014
Values that count as True are called truthy.

00:04:15.014 --> 00:04:18.114
Values that count as False are called falsy.

00:04:18.901 --> 00:04:21.428
Now let's use these questions.

00:04:21.428 --> 00:04:26.821
An if statement has a condition, a colon, and an indented block.

00:04:27.171 --> 00:04:29.448
Python evaluates the condition.

00:04:29.448 --> 00:04:31.578
If it's True, the block runs.

00:04:31.578 --> 00:04:34.148
If not, the whole block is skipped.

00:04:34.148 --> 00:04:39.731
Either way, the program then carries on with the first line after the block.

00:04:40.331 --> 00:04:41.859
Watch the arrow.

00:04:41.859 --> 00:04:44.726
It marks the next line to run.

00:04:44.726 --> 00:04:46.636
The variable x is 5.

00:04:46.636 --> 00:04:48.834
Python prints Before 5.

00:04:48.834 --> 00:04:55.331
5 equals 5 is True, so it steps into the block and runs all 3 lines.

00:04:55.331 --> 00:04:57.051
Then Afterwards 5.

00:04:57.401 --> 00:04:58.680
Then Before 6.

00:04:58.680 --> 00:05:04.255
5 equals 6 is False, so the arrow jumps over the whole block.

00:05:04.255 --> 00:05:08.276
All 3 lines are skipped, not just the first.

00:05:08.276 --> 00:05:09.921
Then Afterwards 6.

00:05:10.701 --> 00:05:14.941
How does Python know which lines belong to the block?

00:05:14.941 --> 00:05:16.461
By the indentation.

00:05:16.461 --> 00:05:19.501
Python uses indentation, not brackets.

00:05:19.851 --> 00:05:24.082
Increase the indent after a line that ends with a colon.

00:05:24.082 --> 00:05:27.784
Keep the same indent for every line of the block.

00:05:27.784 --> 00:05:30.051
Decrease the indent to end it.

00:05:30.651 --> 00:05:34.333
Use 4 spaces per level, and let your editor insert them.

00:05:34.333 --> 00:05:38.411
Blank lines, and comments on a line of their own, don't count.

00:05:38.761 --> 00:05:41.510
And indentation isn't decoration.

00:05:41.510 --> 00:05:45.341
These 2 programs differ only in the last line.

00:05:45.341 --> 00:05:49.006
On the left, print Done is inside the block.

00:05:49.006 --> 00:05:54.921
With x equal to 3, it never runs, so the program prints nothing at all.

00:05:55.271 --> 00:06:00.528
On the right, the line that prints Done is after the block, so it always runs.

00:06:00.528 --> 00:06:02.551
Same lines, different meaning.

00:06:03.334 --> 00:06:05.457
A block can contain another if.

00:06:05.457 --> 00:06:07.648
That's called a nested decision.

00:06:07.648 --> 00:06:11.894
The inner if is only reached when the outer condition is True.

00:06:12.244 --> 00:06:17.403
With x equal to 42: 42 is more than 1, so we go in.

00:06:17.403 --> 00:06:21.348
42 is less than 100, so we go in again.

00:06:21.348 --> 00:06:22.764
Then Finished.

00:06:23.114 --> 00:06:27.215
Read the indentation as invisible begin and end marks.

00:06:27.215 --> 00:06:33.594
Each block begins after the colon, and ends at the first line that is indented less.

00:06:33.944 --> 00:06:35.730
But don't nest too deep.

00:06:35.730 --> 00:06:39.078
5 or 6 levels is very hard to read and check.

00:06:39.078 --> 00:06:44.584
Usually an elif chain, or a logical operator, gives you a flatter program.

00:06:45.367 --> 00:06:48.272
Often you want one thing when the condition is True,

00:06:48.272 --> 00:06:50.227
and something else when it's False.

00:06:50.227 --> 00:06:51.567
That's what else is for.

00:06:51.917 --> 00:06:54.190
It's a fork in the road.

00:06:54.190 --> 00:06:57.316
Exactly one of the 2 blocks runs.

00:06:57.316 --> 00:07:00.157
Never both, and never neither.

00:07:00.757 --> 00:07:04.316
With x equal to 1, 1 greater than 2 is False,

00:07:04.316 --> 00:07:07.717
so the else block runs, and prints Smaller.

00:07:08.067 --> 00:07:10.267
Else has no condition of its own.

00:07:10.267 --> 00:07:14.067
And it must line up with its if, at the same indentation.

00:07:14.834 --> 00:07:19.904
When there are more than 2 paths, use elif, spelled e, l, i, f.

00:07:19.904 --> 00:07:21.754
It's short for else if.

00:07:22.104 --> 00:07:26.493
Python tests the conditions in order, from top to bottom,

00:07:26.493 --> 00:07:30.188
and runs the block of the first one that's True.

00:07:30.188 --> 00:07:33.344
The later conditions are not even tested.

00:07:33.944 --> 00:07:35.340
Here x is 56.

00:07:35.340 --> 00:07:37.058
Less than 2? No.

00:07:37.058 --> 00:07:38.884
Less than 10? No.

00:07:38.884 --> 00:07:43.072
Less than 20? Less than 40? No, and no.

00:07:43.072 --> 00:07:47.904
So the else block runs, and we get Ginormous.

00:07:48.254 --> 00:07:49.751
The else is optional.

00:07:49.751 --> 00:07:53.814
Without it, nothing is printed when no condition is True.

00:07:54.601 --> 00:07:56.884
Pause the video and predict.

00:07:56.884 --> 00:08:03.081
What does this chain print when x is 15? When x is 2? And when x is minus 7?

00:08:03.631 --> 00:08:07.041
When x is 15, the first 2 tests fail,

00:08:07.041 --> 00:08:09.160
but 15 is less than 20.

00:08:09.160 --> 00:08:10.911
So it prints Large.

00:08:11.261 --> 00:08:16.129
For 2, the first test is 2 less than 2, which is False.

00:08:16.129 --> 00:08:19.581
2 is less than 10, so it prints Medium.

00:08:19.931 --> 00:08:25.703
And minus 7 is less than 2, so it prints Small, and nothing else.

00:08:25.703 --> 00:08:29.255
Minus 7 is also less than 10, 20 and 40,

00:08:29.255 --> 00:08:32.451
but only the first True branch runs.

00:08:33.234 --> 00:08:35.402
That gives us a useful pattern.

00:08:35.402 --> 00:08:41.834
In an elif chain, a later branch only sees the inputs that every earlier condition rejected.

00:08:42.184 --> 00:08:47.993
So elif x less than 20 really means: x is at least 10, and less than 20.

00:08:47.993 --> 00:08:51.784
You never need to write both ends of the range.

00:08:52.134 --> 00:08:53.948
Now look at this program.

00:08:53.948 --> 00:08:57.574
Which message can never be printed, whatever x is?

00:08:58.124 --> 00:08:58.900
Below 10.

00:08:58.900 --> 00:09:02.087
Any number below 10 is also below 20,

00:09:02.087 --> 00:09:06.223
so the branch for less than 20 catches it first.

00:09:06.223 --> 00:09:09.324
The fix is to swap the 2 elif lines.

00:09:10.101 --> 00:09:11.501
Let's sum up.

00:09:11.851 --> 00:09:15.166
A Boolean expression is True or False.

00:09:15.166 --> 00:09:17.958
Two equals signs ask a question.

00:09:17.958 --> 00:09:20.051
One equals sign assigns.

00:09:20.401 --> 00:09:23.870
An if runs its block only when the condition is True,

00:09:23.870 --> 00:09:27.601
and the indentation decides which lines are in the block.

00:09:27.951 --> 00:09:33.545
In an if, elif, else chain, the conditions are tested from the top,

00:09:33.545 --> 00:09:36.551
and only the first True branch runs.

00:09:36.901 --> 00:09:38.796
Here's one to try.

00:09:38.796 --> 00:09:45.007
This program should print A for 90 or more, B for 80 to 89,

00:09:45.007 --> 00:09:48.271
C for 60 to 79, and F below 60.

00:09:48.271 --> 00:09:51.956
But for a score of 95, it prints C.

00:09:51.956 --> 00:09:56.061
Why? Fix it by reordering the branches.

00:09:56.611 --> 00:10:00.899
In part 2, we'll combine questions with and, or and not,

00:10:00.899 --> 00:10:03.731
and catch errors with try and except.

